 To find the smallest positive solutions to the equation $x-\lfloor x\rfloor=\frac1{\lfloor x\rfloor}$, we first let $x-\lfloor x\rfloor=y,\;0\le y<1$. Therefore, we have $y=\frac1n\in\mathbb{Q}^+\Rightarrow n=1/y\in\mathbb{N}^*$, and $y=\{x\}$.
</think> 
If $y=\{x\}$, then 
\begin{align*}
x-y&=\lfloor x\rfloor\\
x-\left\{x\right\}&=\lfloor x\rfloor\\
x&:=\lfloor x\rfloor+\{x\}\\
&=\left(y+\frac{1}{y}\right)+y\\
\end{align*}
Now we find the three smallest solutions.
$\boxed{1}$ Let $y=\frac{1}{3}$. Then 
\begin{align*}
x&=\left(y+\frac{1}{y}\right)+y\\
&=\left(\frac{1}{3}+3\right)+\frac{1}{3}\\
&=\boxed{\frac{13}{3}}
\end{align*}
$\boxed{\frac{2}{3}}$ Let $y=\frac{2}{3}$. Then 
\begin{align*}
x&=\left(y+\frac{1}{y}\right)+y\\
&=\left(\frac{2}{3}+\frac{3}{2}\right)+\frac{2}{3}\\
&=\boxed{\frac{19}{6}}
\end{align*}
$\boxed{1}$ Let $y=1$. Then 
\begin{align*}
x&=\left(y+\frac{1}{y}\right)+y\\
&=(1+1)+1\\
&=\boxed{3}
\end{align*}

